WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.449
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:02.449 --> 00:00:07.759
Humans have an uncanny ability to make out
shapes and faces in the dark that aren’t

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:08.930
really there.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:13.440
We do this because our brain is deprived of
visual information to form a complete picture,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.519
because, well…

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:15.599
it’s dark.

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:18.400
So our brain simply fills in the blanks without
telling us.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.400
Cheers brain, thanks for keeping me up at
night wondering why my wall clock suddenly

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.400
looks like a creature from the X-Files.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.900
But the fact that we see things in the dark
that aren’t really there, isn’t the reason

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.289
we’re afraid of the black of the night.

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:29.310
So what is?

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:35.270
Let’s find out.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.060
We are most afraid of the dark when we’re
four to six years old.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:45.370
This fear tapers off by the time we reach
the age of nine, but it never truly goes away.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:50.030
Some adults are considerably more afraid of
the dark than others, but there’s a part

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:55.489
of all of us that has an apprehension towards
dark places.

00:00:55.489 --> 00:01:01.940
In a poll, 40% of adults admitted to feeling
scared walking around their own home in the

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.750
dark.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:08.749
And 10% of adults are too afraid of the dark
to even get out of bed if the lights aren’t

00:01:08.749 --> 00:01:09.749
on.

00:01:09.749 --> 00:01:13.329
A severe fear of the dark is called nyctophobia.

00:01:13.329 --> 00:01:17.560
If you’re particularly afraid of the dark,
you might think it could help to sleep with

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.840
the lights on.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.789
But you may unwittingly be doing harm to your
brain.

00:01:22.789 --> 00:01:28.850
A study at Ohio State University discovered
that sleeping with the lights on, damages

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.740
your brain and increases the likelihood of
depression.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:37.359
This is because when its dark our brain produces
a hormone called Melatonin.

00:01:37.359 --> 00:01:42.990
Created in the pineal gland, melatonin helps
to regulate your body’s sleep cycle.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:48.349
When we don’t get enough sleep or sleep
in a room that isn’t entirely dark, our

00:01:48.349 --> 00:01:51.450
melatonin production is severely inhibited.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:56.520
Over time a lack of melatonin can have negative
consequences on our body.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.999
Such as: Your brain will age faster.

00:02:00.999 --> 00:02:05.420
Melatonin depravation speeds up the rate at
which your brain ages.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:10.179
Researchers think that the elderly often have
more trouble sleeping because their brain

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:14.209
doesn’t produce as much melatonin as younger
individuals.

00:02:14.209 --> 00:02:18.209
Which may also contribute to the degradation
of brain function that some people suffer

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:19.350
from, during old age.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:26.959
A lack of melatonin can also lead to a condition
known as S.A.D., short for Seasonal Affective

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:27.959
Disorder.

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:30.470
SAD is associated with sufferers of depression.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.739
Sufferers of SAD have an altered schedule
for melatonin release.

00:02:34.739 --> 00:02:40.569
SAD suffers don’t produce melatonin until
later on in their sleep cycle, meaning they

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:43.450
get less melatonin overall.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:51.510
This can eventually lead to worsening depression,
bipolar disorder, eating disorders and schizophrenia.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:56.319
Melatonin deprivation has also been linked
to increases in blood pressure and cancer

00:02:56.319 --> 00:02:57.319
rates.

00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:02.810
Health concerns aside, it’s a damn good
job that we are afraid of the dark, and you

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:08.909
shouldn’t be ashamed of it, because believe
it or not, being scared has its advantages.

00:03:08.909 --> 00:03:11.310
Especially when it’s dark.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:15.940
Human kind’s fear of the darkness stems
from the days of early humanity, long before

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.090
we evolved into modern humans.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:22.400
You see, humans haven’t always been at the
top of the food chain.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:27.200
Before the advent of the hunter-gatherer,
and for quite a while afterwards, us humans

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.430
were the hunted ones.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:34.810
Our earliest human-like ancestors first appeared
around seven million years ago.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:38.790
Although, humans didn’t first step foot
off the African continent and into the Middle

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:42.150
East, until 1.75 million years ago.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:47.049
That’s a long, long time that humans had
to defend themselves against the myriad of

00:03:47.049 --> 00:03:51.900
natural threats that they shared the African
planes with.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.200
Until we learnt to defend ourselves, humans
were under constant threat from animals such

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:00.200
as lions, hippos, crocodiles, rhinos and snakes.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:05.510
Our early human ancestors didn’t stand a
chance against a pride of lions, without modern

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.810
tools or shelter.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:11.760
This problem was made even worse during the
night.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.889
Predators used the pitch black of the night
to their advantage.

00:04:14.889 --> 00:04:21.079
A lonely defenseless human wandering the planes
of Africa didn’t stand a chance in the dark.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:27.210
After all, lions and other predators can see
in the dark considerably better than humans.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:34.660
In fact, 60% of lion attacks in Tanzania between
1988 and 2009 were between the dark hours

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:38.130
of 6pm and 9:45pm.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:42.010
Because of this, humans developed an instinctive
fear of the dark.

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:47.020
That feeling of anxiousness you get when you’re
trekking through a dark place is an ancient

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:53.120
reaction that humans have developed to warn
us, that dark places are dangerous.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:59.060
Especially so, because in the dark, we can’t
see where we’re going and we have no idea

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:03.169
what hostile creature might be coming towards
us.

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:10.520
The emotion that is anxiety is just a tool
our body uses to get the job done and that

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.199
job is keeping us safe in the dark.

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:19.430
Over millions of years this fear has been
fine tuned by both nature and nurture to keep

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:21.990
us safe from the perils of the night.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:26.330
You see it’s not just natural selection
that enforces this fear.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:30.052
Millions of years of children seeing their
parents become afraid when going out at night,

00:05:30.052 --> 00:05:31.379
and more recently, mother’s telling their
children not to go out past eight o’clock,

00:05:31.379 --> 00:05:37.220
has helped to reinforce and improve human
kind’s ability to stay anxious and fearful

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.919
of the dark.

00:05:38.919 --> 00:05:43.130
This ability may not be as relevant nowadays,
because we no longer have to contend with

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:46.330
ferocious wild animals on a daily basis.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:53.330
But even in our modern world, the night still
holds many threats, mostly of the human variety.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:58.639
So we should be very thankful for our fear
of the dark, because it offers at least a

00:05:58.639 --> 00:06:07.750
modicum of protection, constantly reminding
us not to tread where danger may lurk.

